  "enhanced_information": "The 12-year-old boy presents with fever, weakness, periorbital erythema, edema, ophthalmoplegia, and proptosis, suggesting a possible orbital cellulitis or cavernous sinus involvement due to recent facial trauma. In cases of orbital cellulitis, cranial nerve involvement can lead to sensory deficits. The most likely sequela on the affected side is anesthesia along the CN V3 distribution, as this nerve is responsible for sensation in the lower face and can be affected by inflammation or compression in the orbital region. Other options, such as decreased vision, monocular diplopia, and absent blink reflex, may occur but are less directly supported by the clinical presentation and consensus on the sequelae of orbital cellulitis. Therefore, the best-supported finding is anesthesia along the CN V3 distribution."
